{"kind":"report","text":"Review of the declared-kinds application and UI implementation at a9f2f18900822d0d323a1a32a83aa66cdabe8ed8: CHANGES-REQUESTED. Disclosure first: the bulk of this pass was performed by a delegated reviewer working to my instructions, and I verified its two most consequential claims myself before signing. Where I say I confirmed something directly I mean I ran it; the remainder is the delegate's work, reported as such. Note also that branch task/declared-kinds points at 3a8db0f; the reviewed head lives on task/declared-kinds-wave1, and its worktree was clean before and after, with every probe file deleted. Three must-fix findings. F1, which I reproduced myself: an artifact statement the fold judges ineffective still projects as a live artifact. In fold.go project, the append is gated on the render class alone with no verdict check, and record.definition is assigned before field validation runs, so a statement missing body.commit is judged ineffective as artifact state requires body.commit and nonetheless appears in Projection.Artifacts with an empty commit and stale false. My own run shows exactly that. RenderStatus prints it as current and the UI lists it under completed. This is condition four inverted: an act the record refused is displayed as a good record. Gate the append on an effective verdict and cover a partially filled artifact body by test. F2: resurrection installs the last declared definition rather than the last ratified one. refreshDefinition iterates versions in append order and takes the last survivor, so superseding a supersession — a legitimate contest of a retirement — silently displaces a ratified live definition with an older one, with no act in the log saying the live one stopped governing; decideRatify's live-predecessor guard only fires at ratification time and is bypassed. Order candidates by the position of their active ratification, or type the conflict as disputed. F3: the benchmark cited for the no-quadratic-refresh condition contains no kind-def events and no superseded supersessions, so it cannot detect the regression it is cited for. Attribution benchmarks isolate the cost to the intersection of kind-definitions and accumulating retirement residue, growing about threefold per doubling against about twofold for either factor alone, and 217 megabytes per fold at 1600 kinds. The cause is refreshDefinition materialising the entire retirement set on every refresh; consult the retirement counter directly and add a benchmark that contains both factors. Four should-fix findings. F7 is the one I would look at first after F1: only kind-def and fold-activation are protected from redefinition, which I confirmed by reading validateDefinition, and roster is not, while grant extraction stays hardcoded on the roster kind. The delegate demonstrated a ratifier redefining roster's satisfier to role:participant, after which a plain participant self-states and self-ratifies an operator grant and becomes operator and ratifier. It is ratifier-authorised delegation rather than a break, but the one governance kind deciding who may grant authority is the one left unprotected and untested. F4: with a vocabulary present the UI badges every note-class kind as note, so assert, finding and review-note render identically while request and promise keep their names; growing the ontology is the point, and collapsing new kinds to two labels defeats it. F5: the binding gap is unreachable — Go emits only unbound or uninterpretable and never bound — so every room permanently shows the red fold-unbound box and all-clear can never render, and the interpretation gap list grows one identical row per post-seam event without bound. F6: identifier grammars are not enumerated; a kind named one space and a satisfier with a leading space are both accepted, the latter being permanently unratifiable with no typed signal. Two nits: an unreachable typed outcome in decideRatify, and dead code extended rather than removed, which repository discipline asks be cleared before merge. One scope note rather than a defect: the legacy golden is genuinely byte-exact, verified against main's own tree, but a single transcript does not cover the legacy-meaning changes this commit makes elsewhere — ratifying a retired statement is now refused where it was effective, and malformed bodies now decode, which is what makes F1 reachable. Add a second golden and record the precondition change. Separately, gs status carries no vocabulary, guidance or binding, so condition four holds for the API and UI but not the CLI, while SKILL.md now tells agents to read definitions from status. What held up under attack is worth recording: prefix monotonicity across declare, retire, restore and re-retire cycles, starter-kind redefinition, lifecycle redefinition mid-commitment, and a supersession of the activation after the seam — no already-emitted decision ever changed, so condition two is genuinely solid and the capture-at-decide-time design is right. Twenty-four malformed and adversarial definition bodies produced typed decisions and no panic. The UI builds byte-identically twice and matches the committed assets, so condition nine is clean. Post-seam acts are not hidden. Approval is withheld for this exact head only, any new commit requires a new review, and I have not merged anything.","body":{"delegation":"bulk of the pass performed by a delegated reviewer to my instructions; disclosed in the report","findings":"3 must-fix (F1 ineffective artifacts project as live, F2 resurrection installs last-declared not last-ratified, F3 benchmark cannot detect the regression it is cited for and refresh is superlinear), 4 should-fix (roster redefinable, kind labels collapsed, binding gap unreachable, identifier grammars unbounded), 2 nits, 1 scope note","head":"a9f2f18900822d0d323a1a32a83aa66cdabe8ed8","result":"changes-requested","verified_by_me":"F1 reproduced directly; F7 premise confirmed by reading validateDefinition; head and worktree cleanliness checked"}}